if err := gateway.PrecheckInline(n); err != nil { return err }: var ce *CodeError, maybe assigned, return ce.
Run-time truth: a non-nil *CodeError (the literal) or a typed nil *CodeError; both are non-nil errors.
Expect 1 entry: concrete *CodeError at the conversion "return ce", which stands for both. It carries no
[typed nil] mark (the mark is only given to a pointer that is always nil) and its span is the returned
identifier, not the literal. An empty set would be unsound; contrast nil_check_typed_nil.
